.TH MSTSERVER "1" "September 2026" "mstflint" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mstserver \- serve local devices to remote mstflint tools over HTTPS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mstserver start
[\fB\-p\fR \fIport\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIpassphrase\fR]
.br
.B mstserver stop
[\fB\-p\fR \fIport\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mstserver
exposes the devices of the host it runs on to mstflint tools running elsewhere.
Clients register those devices with
.BR mstremote (1)
and then address them as
\fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR,\fIdevice\fR, for example
.IP
mstflint \-d myhost:8080,0000:43:00.0 q
.PP
.B start
detaches and runs in the background, recording its process id in
\fI/var/run/mst/mstserver.<port>.pid\fR.
.B stop
signals that process and removes the file.
Both require root: the server opens devices directly.
.PP
Access is authenticated. Every request other than the health probe requires a
session token, which clients obtain with the passphrase. If
.B \-s
is not given, a random passphrase is generated and printed once at start; there
is no unauthenticated mode. Tokens are held in memory only, so restarting the
server invalidates them and clients must run
.B mstremote add
again.
.SS Transport security
Traffic is TLS. On its first start the server generates a P\-256 key pair under
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_server\fR and issues itself a certificate for it; the
key is then reused for the life of the installation. The certificate is not
signed by any authority, so clients do not validate a chain: they pin the
public key instead, the way
.BR ssh (1)
pins a host key.
.PP
The fingerprint of that key is printed at every start. It is what a client shows
when it first connects to this server, and comparing the two out of band is what
makes the client's question worth answering. It can be re\-read at any time by
running
.B mstserver start
again, or from the certificate with
.IP
openssl x509 \-in /var/lib/mstflint/mst_server/server.crt \-pubkey \-noout |
.br
openssl pkey \-pubin \-outform der | openssl dgst \-sha256 \-binary |
.br
openssl base64
.PP
The key must outlive restarts, reinstalls of the certificate, and changes of
hostname: every client that has connected refuses a server whose key has
changed, and an operator has to clear the stale entry by hand. Deleting
\fIserver.key\fR is therefore not a routine operation.
.PP
The passphrase travels inside the TLS session. The server keeps only a salted
PBKDF2\-HMAC\-SHA256 verifier of it, never the passphrase or a digest that
could be replayed in its place.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR \fIport\fR
TCP port to listen on. The default is 8080. One server may run per port; the
process id file is named after the port.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-passphrase\fR \fIpassphrase\fR
Session passphrase. When omitted a random one is generated and printed. Note
that an argument is visible in \fBps\fR(1) and recorded in the shell history.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show usage and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start on the default port and register the devices from another host:
.IP
# mstserver start
.br
\-I\- Passphrase for this session:
.br
\-I\-     0ad7ab253962c94de05194ecc27fe432
.br
\-I\- Server key fingerprint:
.br
\-I\-     SHA256:2YMOeTLaODASHO/h44tV8KPGg4xG1X+TOQIZVjC2ghA
.PP
On the client, which asks you to confirm that same fingerprint:
.IP
# mstremote add myhost:8080 \-s 0ad7ab253962c94de05194ecc27fe432
.PP
Stop it:
.IP
# mstserver stop
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI/var/run/mst/mstserver.<port>.pid\fR
Process id of the running server.
.TP
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_server/server.key\fR
The server's private key, owner\-readable only. Its public half is what clients
pin.
.TP
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_server/server.crt\fR
Self\-signed certificate for that key. Re\-issued automatically if it is
removed or stops matching the key.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MST_SERVER_TLS_DIR
Directory holding the key and certificate, instead of
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_server\fR. Intended for testing; a server started with
it has a different identity from one started without.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B mstremote(1), mstflint(1)
